processors are already broadly useful machines.\",\n      \"heading\": \"Near-term scientific role\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"body\": \"Highly entangled quantum dynamics is presented as the most natural arena for meaningful quantum advantage, and hybrid QAOA or VQE approaches remain plausible NISQ-era testbeds. At the same time, the evidence does not support expecting near-term quantum computers to efficiently solve hard NP-hard optimization instances, and the random-circuit speedup does not by itself establish general application value.\",\n      \"heading\": \"Plausible and uncertain applications\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"body\": \"Both sources separate near-term noisy demonstrations from the longer-term goal of scalable fault-tolerant quantum computing. Preskill emphasizes the heavy physical-qubit overhead of quantum error correction, while the Sycamore source notes that applications such as Shor-style factoring still require fault-tolerant logical qubits.\",\n      \"heading\": \"Fault-tolerant horizon\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"source_ids\": [\n    \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n    \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\"\n  ],\n  \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n  \"title\": \"Near-Term Quantum Value Versus Fault-Tolerant Promise\",\n  \"topic_id\": \"topic-near-term-value-versus-fault-tolerant-promise\"\n}\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
